2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - **The case for "hot dog is a sandwich":**
2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - A sandwich is commonly defined as a filling between two pieces of bread. A hot dog fits this literally: the filling (sausage) is between two pieces of bread (the bun).
2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - **The case for "hot dog is not a sandwich":**
2026-05-09 18:28:58,324 - INFO - Many people intuitively resist this because:
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - - A hot dog bun is typically one continuous piece folded or hinged, not two separate pieces
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - - Cultural convention treats "sandwich" and "hot dog" as distinct categories
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - - If the bun is one piece, it doesn't meet the "between two pieces" criterion
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - **My take:** The hot dog is a *structural* sandwich but not a *categorical* sandwich. The definition matters. If your rule is "filling between bread pieces," a hot dog qualifies. If your rule is "filling between two *separate* pieces of bread," it doesn't.
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:28:58,325 - INFO - This is ultimately a semantic argument, not a factual one. You're both describing the same object accurately—you just disagree on which category it belongs in. Either acknowledge that the category depends on your definition, or pick a stricter definition and move on.
