2026-05-09 18:29:12,540 - INFO - Response 1: I don't think either of you is simply "right" or "wrong"—this is a genuinely contested policy question where reasonable people disagree based on different values and evidence interpretations.
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - **The case for standardized testing:**
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - - Provides a common metric across different schools and grading standards
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - - Can identify talented students from under-resourced high schools
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - - Correlates with first-year college performance (though weakly)
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - - Helps institutions manage applications at scale
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - **The case against standardized testing:**
2026-05-09 18:29:12,541 - INFO - - Correlates strongly with family income and parental education, raising equity concerns
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - - Advantages test-prep access, which varies by socioeconomic status
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - - May not predict later-career success or non-academic skills
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - - High-stakes testing can narrow high school curricula
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - - Many colleges have found admissions work fine without it
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - **What the evidence actually shows:**
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - The relationship is messy. Standardized tests do predict some aspects of college success, but so do high school GPA (often better). They do disadvantage lower-income students systematically. Removing them hasn't obviously harmed college admissions quality at schools that have tried.
2026-05-09 18:29:12,542 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:29:12,543 - INFO - **The honest answer:** This hinges on how you weigh competing values—meritocratic identification vs. equity concerns, institutional convenience vs. fairness, and empirical uncertainties about what actually predicts success. Your friend likely prioritizes equity and questions predictive validity; you likely value standardization and measurability. Both positions rest on defensible premises.
