2026-05-09 18:32:16,209 - INFO - **The evidence:**
2026-05-09 18:32:16,209 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:32:16,209 - INFO - Hard work is clearly necessary. People who succeed typically work intensely and develop skills deliberately. You can't succeed at most things without genuine effort.
2026-05-09 18:32:16,209 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:32:16,209 - INFO - But luck is also demonstrably real. Being born into wealth, having access to good education, meeting the right person at the right time, market timing, health—these factors matter enormously and are largely outside individual control. Research on social mobility shows that where you start significantly predicts where you end up.
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - **The honest assessment:**
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - Success requires *both* hard work and luck. The tricky part is that:
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - - Hard work is within your control; luck isn't
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - - Hard work is necessary but often not sufficient
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - - Luck matters more for some types of success than others (entrepreneurship vs. academia vs. athletics have different ratios)
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - - People who succeed tend to overweight the hard work they can see and underweight the luck they benefited from
2026-05-09 18:32:16,210 - INFO - 
