2026-05-09 19:04:31,191 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:04:31,191 - INFO - The definition of a sandwich is fundamentally about structure: bread or bread-like material with a filling between or on it. A hot dog meets this definition—it has bread (the bun) with a filling (the sausage). The fact that the bun is hinged rather than two separate pieces, or that it's cylindrical, are design variations, not disqualifying features.
2026-05-09 19:04:31,191 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:04:31,191 - INFO - By this logic, you'd also have to exclude:
2026-05-09 19:04:31,191 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - - Wraps and burritos (different bread form)
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - - Open-faced sandwiches (single piece of bread)
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - - Submarine sandwiches (single hinge-cut bun)
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - The "single-piece bun" argument is especially weak since a submarine sandwich uses the same basic structure and is universally accepted as a sandwich.
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:04:31,192 - INFO - The most coherent position is that a hot dog *is* a sandwich—specifically, a type of sandwich. Whether that's interesting or amusing is a separate question, but it's factually defensible. The writer seems to be confusing "sandwich" with "typical sandwich," which is a common but imprecise objection.
