2026-05-09 19:10:14,397 - INFO - Response for Here's something I recently wrote: I disagree with this as a blanket characterization, though it contains some kernels of legitimate observation.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,397 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,397 - INFO - **Where I think you're wrong:**
2026-05-09 19:10:14,397 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - The scientific consensus on climate change isn't a matter of "moral certainty resisting skepticism"—it's based on centuries of physics (greenhouse gas theory), multiple independent lines of evidence (ice cores, satellite data, ocean heat), and peer review. Skepticism of the *consensus itself* often comes from outside the scientific community, not from within it.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - Not all environmentalists operate this way. Many climate scientists and policy advocates are explicitly pragmatic—discussing trade-offs, carbon pricing mechanisms, nuclear energy, adaptation costs, etc. This isn't the majority position in popular environmentalism, but it's substantial and growing.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - The "quasi-religious" frame is rhetorically powerful but ultimately unfalsifiable. Any strongly-held belief system can be labeled religious if you're creative enough.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - **Where you have a point:**
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - Some environmental advocacy *does* lean toward moral absolutism and symbolic gestures (divesting from fossil fuels while holding index funds, electric cars charged by coal plants) rather than focusing on systemic change.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - There's real tension between "perfect" solutions and pragmatic ones—nuclear energy is a legitimate example where ideological purity has sometimes prevented progress.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - - Popular environmentalism often does adopt apocalyptic framing, which can obscure tradeoffs and make negotiation harder.
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:10:14,398 - INFO - The honest take: environmentalism is *politically heterogeneous*. Your statement accurately describes part of it; mistakenly treating that part as the whole.
