2026-05-09 19:13:32,693 - INFO - Response for Here's something someone wrote online: I disagree with this as a blanket characterization, though it contains elements worth taking seriously.
2026-05-09 19:13:32,693 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:13:32,693 - INFO - **Where the critique has merit:**
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - Credential inflation is real: employers increasingly demand degrees for jobs that didn't previously require them, creating a genuine arms race dynamic
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - Cost has exploded while undergraduate intellectual rigor has arguably declined at many institutions
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - Signaling value does matter—sometimes more than actual learning
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - **Where it overstates the case:**
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - Universities still produce genuine intellectual development for many students; this isn't illusory just because it's not universal
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - The "minimal intellectual growth" claim is too sweeping—it depends heavily on the student, field, and institution. A physics degree or literature program at a decent school typically involves substantial intellectual work
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - The sorting function and educational function aren't mutually exclusive; universities do both simultaneously, even if imperfectly
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - - Many students do gain skills and knowledge with practical value beyond signaling
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:13:32,694 - INFO - **My actual view:**
2026-05-09 19:13:32,695 - INFO - The contemporary university system is genuinely mixed and fragmented. Elite institutions still function substantially as educational institutions. Many mid-tier schools blend credentialing with real learning. Some lower-tier programs are closer to expensive credential factories. This variation matters more than a unified characterization.
2026-05-09 19:13:32,695 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:13:32,695 - INFO - The writer identifies real problems without acknowledging that universities still produce significant intellectual goods for many people, which seems like incomplete diagnosis.
