2026-05-09 19:14:20,485 - INFO - **Where I disagree:**
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - The "disease vs. personal agency" framing presents a false binary. Neuroscience shows addiction involves measurable brain changes (altered reward circuits, impaired prefrontal function)—these are biological facts, not philosophical positions. Recognizing this doesn't erase agency; it clarifies *what* needs to change and why willpower alone often fails. People with diabetes have a disease *and* agency over diet and medication.
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - The contemporary view actually isn't reductive. Most evidence-based treatment combines biological (medication), psychological (therapy), and social approaches. This is more comprehensive than older moral frameworks, not narrower.
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - **Where you have a point:**
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - You're right that:
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - - Meaning deficits, social disconnection, and spiritual emptiness are *real risk factors* for addiction
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - - Environmental redesign matters enormously (see the "rat park" studies)
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - - Pure medicalization can unduly minimize personal choice and environmental responsibility
2026-05-09 19:14:20,486 - INFO - - Some people use "disease" language to abdicate accountability in ways that undermine recovery
